-- Passage rg10-character-setting-plot-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ad335b15-aa6a-592a-96df-74695b05d564', 'rg10-character-setting-plot-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Margins$q$, $q$Noor Rahimi needed six more service hours by Friday, and the county historical society had promised that transcribing old payroll ledgers was the fastest way to earn them. The job was simple. Each page of the Harlow Mill ledger, dated 1891, had been scanned, and volunteers typed every worker's name and weekly wage into a spreadsheet. Noor typed fast. On her first afternoon she finished nine pages, and the volunteer coordinator wrote "excellent pace" on her sign-in sheet, which pleased her more than she expected.

Her partner at the long table was Mr. Abernathy, a retired machinist who had been transcribing the same ledger since spring. He was on page forty. Noor had already passed him.

"You skip the margins," he said on the second afternoon, without looking up.

"The instructions say names and wages only," Noor answered. She did not add that margins slowed her down.

"The instructions were written by someone in a hurry." He turned his screen toward her. In faded brown ink beside the name Ruth Callender, a clerk had written: Docked half day, child ill. Mr. Abernathy scrolled. The same note appeared three weeks in a row, then a fourth.

Noor looked at it longer than she meant to. She told herself she was only resting her hands. Then she opened her own finished pages and searched for Callender. Ruth appeared on six of them, and on two Noor had typed the wage without the note that explained why it was lower.

She stayed until the building closed, which earned her nothing extra, since her required hours were already logged by then. On Thursday she asked the coordinator whether the society would want a separate column for margin notes. The coordinator said nobody had ever asked, and Noor, to her own surprise, offered to start it.$q$, 'draft')
