   $q$How does the speaker's map in the fourth stanza advance the poem's plot?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It copies the uncle's mistake, showing that the speaker shares his stubbornness.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It offers a solution that keeps the map accurate while still honoring the old bridge.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It replaces the uncle's maps in the shop, so customers stop asking him for changes.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It erases the old bridge completely, forcing the uncle to accept that it is gone.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It offers a solution that keeps the map accurate while still honoring the old bridge.$q$, $q$The speaker inks the new bridge "where it really stands" and keeps the old one in "light pencil," joining accuracy and memory. The old bridge is not erased; it is drawn as something "you are not ready to erase."$q$, 'draft', '6a053eac-0bc2-5347-a207-b0e9e427a623')
