-- Passage rg10-comparing-texts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('59b3cd2c-92ca-524f-9823-2f13b7c6d076', 'rg10-comparing-texts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Holding On and Letting Go$q$, $q$Text 1: The Kite That Hated Its String (an invented fable)

A paper kite lived on a hill where the wind blew every afternoon. Each day a girl ran with it until it rose above the trees, and each day the kite strained against the string in her hand. "If not for this cord," the kite complained to a passing swallow, "I could climb to the clouds." The swallow tilted its wings and said nothing.

One gusty afternoon the string snapped. For a moment the kite soared higher than ever, and it laughed. Then the wind shifted, the kite spun without direction, and it fell into a thornbush at the bottom of the hill. The kite learned too late that the string it hated was the very thing that held it up.

Text 2: Release (a story)

For four months, Yusra Haddad and her physics club had built the payload: a foam box holding a camera, a temperature sensor and a small radio beacon. On launch morning they filled a weather balloon with helium in the school parking lot, and Yusra held the line while her teacher, Mr. Kowalczyk, checked the battery one last time.

Unlike a kite, this balloon was meant to be let go. It would rise for nearly two hours, burst in the thin cold air and float back down on a parachute. The camera would record the curve of the horizon. The only thing connecting the club to their project would be the beacon, beeping its location to a receiver in the teacher's car.

Yusra's hands tightened on the line. Letting go felt like losing it. Then she realized that the real connection had never been the cord in her fingers. It was the signal, and the months of planning that told them where to look. She opened her hand. The balloon climbed, shrank to a white dot and vanished.

Three hours later the club found the payload in a soybean field forty miles east, exactly where the beacon said it would be. The footage showed a blue sky darkening nearly to black. Yusra decided that freedom and connection were not opposites after all. They just needed the right kind of string.$q$, 'draft')
