-- Passage rg10-comparing-texts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ff8b5c12-6e50-5a5a-b4b8-08011d52b932', 'rg10-comparing-texts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Keepers of Time$q$, $q$Text 1: Why the Tortoise Keeps the Calendar (an invented myth)

In an invented myth from a river delta, the first animals argued over who should keep track of the seasons. The hare offered first, boasting that it could count the days faster than anyone. The parrot offered next, promising to announce each new season in a voice the whole forest could hear. The tortoise said only that it would not forget.

The animals tested them. The hare counted so quickly that it lost its place by the third moon. The parrot announced spring twice and forgot summer entirely while it chattered. The tortoise scratched one mark on its shell each day and never skipped one. So the animals gave the calendar to the tortoise, and the myth explains that this is why its shell is covered in small, careful lines.

Text 2: The Courthouse Clock (a story)

Every Tuesday for thirty-one years, Mrs. Eleni Marchetti climbed the narrow stairs of the old courthouse tower in Bellfield to wind the town clock. She did it slowly, one step at a time, and she wrote the date and the clock's error in a small notebook: two seconds fast, one second slow, exactly on time.

When the town council voted to replace the mechanism with an electric motor, several people cheered. The new system would update itself automatically, a council member explained, and no one would need to climb the stairs. The motor was installed in March. By June, a power flicker had reset it four times, and the clock face showed the wrong hour for a whole weekend before anyone noticed.

Mrs. Marchetti's grandson, Theo, found her notebooks while helping her clean the attic. Page after page held tiny, careful entries, thirty-one years of Tuesdays without a gap. He brought them to the next council meeting. The council did not remove the motor, but it asked Mrs. Marchetti to train two high school volunteers to check the clock each week and record what they found, just as she had. Theo signed up first.$q$, 'draft')
