insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('001ee688-f6e0-574b-b339-66b07e59a367', 'rg10-comparing-texts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Promise Kept$q$, $q$Text 1: The Salt Trader's Promise (an invented legend)

An invented legend from a coastal trading route tells of Haruto, a salt trader who promised a far inland village that he would arrive with salt before the first frost every year. The village needed salt to preserve its winter food, and no other trader traveled that far.

For twenty years Haruto kept his word. One autumn his mule went lame, and the roads were thick with mud. Other traders told him that no one could blame him for staying home. Haruto loaded the salt onto his own back, walked the last stretch in eleven days instead of six and reached the village the evening before the frost. The villagers asked why he had not simply sent word that he could not come. "A promise is a road," Haruto said. "If I stop walking it, people stop trusting that it leads anywhere."

Text 2: Trail Report (a story)

In eighth grade, Anjali Brennan built a simple website for her hiking club: a single page listing which trails in Corrow State Forest were muddy, flooded or clear. Without quite meaning to, she added a line at the bottom: "Updated every Friday." By tenth grade the page had readers she had never met. They included a retired couple planning weekend walks, a scout troop leader and a woman who pushed her father's wheelchair along the paved loop and needed to know when it was blocked.

Then Anjali sprained her ankle in October. Her doctor told her to stay off trails for a month, and her friends said nobody would mind if the report skipped a few weeks. Anjali was not so sure. She asked two club members to send her photos from the trailheads, called the ranger station every Thursday and posted the update on Friday as usual, adding a note that this month's report came from "many feet, not mine."

In November a message arrived through the site's contact form. The woman with the wheelchair wrote that she had nearly canceled a visit before checking the page and finding the paved loop clear. Anjali read it twice. She had once thought of the Friday line as a habit. Now it felt more like a promise.$q$, 'draft')
