-- Passage rg10-connections-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0193cde0-e253-51c2-b572-2543941c695a', 'rg10-connections-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Blue of Orsk Hollow$q$, $q$For generations, the potters of the fictional village of Orsk Hollow made plain brown jugs and bowls from the clay that lay along the river. Their work was sturdy and cheap, and it rarely traveled beyond the nearby market towns.

In the spring of 1902, a storm tore the metal damper off the chimney of Linnea Dabrowski's kiln in the middle of a firing. With the damper gone, air rushed through the kiln and the fire burned far hotter than usual. Dabrowski expected to find a ruined load. Instead, the pots on the top shelf had come out a deep, glassy blue.

She spent the next four years trying to make the color again. She varied the wood, the temperature and the length of the firing, and most of her attempts came out brown. Her breakthrough came when she noticed that the blue appeared only on pots made from clay dug at one bend of the river. That clay held more iron than the rest, and the iron turned blue only when the kiln was very hot and the air inside was cut off near the end of the firing.

Once Dabrowski understood the recipe, other potters in the village learned it from her. Buyers from distant cities began to seek out "Orsk blue," and the village grew from a handful of workshops to more than thirty.

That success created its own problem. By the 1950s the iron-rich clay at the river bend was nearly gone. The potters responded by studying the clay's makeup and blending ordinary clay with a measured amount of iron powder. Today the color is made by careful chemistry rather than by luck, but it still carries the name of the village where a storm first revealed it.$q$, 'draft')
