-- Passage rg10-context-clues-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('76b9c90a-7711-534e-a377-a5a088324c96', 'rg10-context-clues-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Flowers That Open After Dark$q$, $q$In the deserts of the Southwest, one cactus keeps an unusual schedule. For most of the year it is dormant: it looks like a cluster of dry, gray sticks, and a hiker could walk right past it without suspecting it was alive. Then, on a single night in early summer, it produces large white flowers that open after sunset and wilt before the next noon.

The plant's timing is no accident. Its chief visitors are nocturnal moths, insects that rest during the heat of the day and feed only after dark. A flower that opened at noon would bake in the sun and attract almost no one. By blooming at night, the cactus meets its pollinators when they are actually searching for food.

Color and scent help as well. In bright sunlight, a white petal might look plain beside the reds and yellows of other desert flowers. In moonlight, however, those same pale petals become conspicuous, standing out so sharply against the dark ground that a moth can spot them from far away. The flowers also release a sweet perfume that drifts across the sand on the cool night air.

The relationship between the moth and the cactus is reciprocal. The moth drinks the flower's nectar, and in return it carries pollen to the next plant, allowing the cactus to produce seeds. Neither partner gives without receiving something back.

For researchers, the blooms are notoriously elusive. Because the flowers last only a few hours and many plants open on the same night, a scientist who arrives a day late may find nothing but withered petals. Some teams now camp near marked plants for weeks, checking them every evening, so they will not miss the brief event.$q$, 'draft')
