-- Passage rg10-context-clues-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f8a6ef5e-e307-5209-a671-71b62019a896', 'rg10-context-clues-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Ice From the Lakes$q$, $q$Before electric refrigerators, keeping food cold in summer was a serious problem. Milk, meat, and fruit are perishable; left in a warm kitchen, they spoil within days or even hours. In the 1800s, an unlikely industry grew up to solve this problem: the harvesting and shipping of natural ice.

Each winter, crews walked onto frozen lakes in the northern United States and cut the ice into enormous blocks. Horses dragged sharp plows that scored the surface in a grid, carving long grooves that guided the workers, who finished each cut with long saws. The blocks were then stacked in tall wooden icehouses. Thick walls and layers of sawdust slowed the flow of warm air so well that much of the ice survived until the following autumn.

At first, many merchants were skeptical. They doubted that ice could travel far without melting, and they refused to invest money in the early shipments. The doubters were proven wrong when packed ice reached ports thousands of miles away with most of its weight still intact.

Once the method was shown to work, the trade became lucrative. Ice that cost almost nothing to cut could be sold in hot cities for many times its price, and some shippers built large fortunes. Icehouses proliferated along rail lines and harbors, multiplying so quickly that by the middle of the century nearly every large town had one.

The early equipment was simple, little more than saws, hooks, and wagons. Yet this system changed how people ate. Families could buy fresh food in larger amounts, and butchers and dairies could serve customers who lived farther away. The trade faded only when machines that manufactured ice became cheap and reliable.$q$, 'draft')
