-- Passage rg10-context-clues-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('06099065-6c2d-5197-af3d-41dd0bd19709', 'rg10-context-clues-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Morning Row$q$, $q$At five-thirty in the morning, the river was so still that it looked like polished metal. Lucia Ferreira climbed into the narrow boat with her seven teammates and settled onto her sliding seat, still half asleep.

Coach Adebayo was a taciturn man who rarely said more than a few words at a time, so the crew paid close attention whenever he spoke. Today he said only one thing before they pushed off: "Feather cleanly." Lucia knew what he meant. After each stroke, a rower turns the oar blade flat so it skims above the water instead of catching the wind. A blade that is not turned flat acts like a small sail and slows the boat.

For the first ten minutes, their rhythm was erratic. One rower rushed up the slide while another lagged behind, and the boat rocked from side to side with every uneven stroke. The coxswain, a small sophomore named Mira, called out the cadence, the steady count that tells every rower when to pull: "Catch, and drive. Catch, and drive."

Slowly the crew began to move as one. Eight blades entered the water at the same instant and left it together. The boat stopped rocking and began to glide, and Lucia felt the strange lightness that rowers chase all season. This cohesion was what Coach Adebayo valued above raw strength. In one of his rare longer speeches, he had told them that a boat full of powerful rowers who pulled at different times would lose to a weaker crew that worked together.

When they returned to the dock, the coach nodded once. For him, that single nod was the equivalent of a long speech of praise.$q$, 'draft')
