-- Passage rg10-context-clues-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('acbb901b-766b-5e28-be9b-b69bfc4d60bb', 'rg10-context-clues-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Bridges That Report on Themselves$q$, $q$Every bridge bends a little. When a loaded truck rolls across, the deck sags by a fraction of a centimeter and then springs back. These movements are imperceptible to drivers, far too small to see or feel, yet over decades they can slowly weaken steel and concrete.

Traditionally, engineers checked bridges by sending inspectors to look for damage every year or two. That approach is reactive: it responds to cracks only after they have already formed and become visible. A growing number of cities now prefer a proactive strategy, one that tries to catch trouble before it can cause harm.

The key tool is a network of small sensors attached to beams and cables. Some sensors measure strain, the amount a material stretches or squeezes under a load. Others record vibration or temperature. The readings travel wirelessly to a computer that stores them and compares each day's numbers with months of earlier data.

Most of the time, the numbers follow a predictable pattern. A bridge stretches more during rush hour and on hot afternoons, then relaxes at night. What the software searches for is an anomaly, a reading that breaks the usual pattern without an obvious reason. A beam that suddenly stretches more on a quiet night, for example, might signal a hidden crack.

The system is not perfect. Signals can be intermittent, dropping out during storms and returning hours later, which leaves gaps in the record. Sensors also cost money to install and maintain. Still, many engineers argue that the expense is small compared with the cost of repairing, or replacing, a bridge whose damage went unnoticed for years.$q$, 'draft')
