   $q$Paragraph 2 contrasts a "reactive" approach with a "proactive" strategy. Based on this contrast, a proactive strategy is one that:$q$, $q$[{"text":"acts early to prevent problems before they happen","is_correct":true},{"text":"relies on inspectors who visit every few years","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-contrast"},{"text":"replaces every old bridge with a newer design","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"responds to damage once it becomes visible","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-contrast"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$acts early to prevent problems before they happen$q$, $q$A proactive strategy "tries to catch trouble before it can cause harm," the opposite of a reactive approach that responds "only after" cracks are visible. Responding to visible damage describes the reactive side of the contrast, not the proactive one.$q$, 'draft', 'acbb901b-766b-5e28-be9b-b69bfc4d60bb')
