insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('20b7ea01-ca15-5251-9b32-403268cf47d7', 'rg10-context-clues-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Nothing in This Room Is in a Hurry$q$, $q$On his first day volunteering in the museum's conservation lab, Yusuf Haddad was handed a pair of cotton gloves and a warning. "Nothing in this room is in a hurry," said Dr. Lindqvist, the head conservator. "So neither are we."

The object on the table was a hand-drawn map of a harbor, nearly two hundred years old. Its paper had grown brittle; when Yusuf lifted one corner too quickly, a tiny flake snapped off and drifted to the table. He froze, embarrassed, but Dr. Lindqvist simply picked up the flake with tweezers and set it in a labeled envelope.

She was meticulous in everything she did. She photographed the map from six angles, measured each tear to the millimeter, and recorded every stain in a notebook before she touched a single fiber. Yusuf, who usually finished his art homework in one rushed evening, found the pace almost unbearable at first.

"Our goal isn't to make it look new," she explained. "A pristine map, perfectly clean and unmarked, would be hiding its own history. The ring in the corner where someone once set down a cup is part of the story. We only want to stop it from deteriorating any further, so it doesn't keep crumbling year after year."

By the end of the month, Yusuf had learned to mend a tear with strips of tissue so thin they were translucent. Light passed through them, and the faded ink lines of the map still showed beneath, softened but readable. When the map finally went back on display, no visitor would notice his repairs. That, Dr. Lindqvist told him, was exactly the point.$q$, 'draft')
