-- Passage rg10-context-clues-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a393645a-b240-5a10-8a03-34fcbda295db', 'rg10-context-clues-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Two Stars Above the Hills$q$, $q$The astronomy club had planned the night for weeks. A rare pair of stars would be visible just above the eastern hills, and Mr. Tran had promised that the club's old telescope could resolve them, separating what looked like a single point of light into two distinct stars.

The trouble began before sunset. Clouds rolled in from the west, and the lights at the nearby stadium filled the sky with ambient glow, the general background light that spreads across a whole area and washes out faint objects. Ines Morales checked the forecast every few minutes, as if refreshing it might change the answer.

By nine o'clock, several members had decided that waiting was futile. "Nothing is going to clear," one of them said as he folded his chair. "We're just sitting in the cold for no reason." Half the group left with him.

Ines stayed. So did Mr. Tran and two seniors who had brought a thermos of hot cocoa. They kept a quiet vigil beside the telescope, watching the sky the way people watch a door they expect to open at any moment. Ines took notes in her logbook to stay awake, recording the temperature and the shape of the clouds every quarter hour.

Just after eleven, the wind shifted. The clouds began to dissipate, thinning and breaking apart until patches of black sky appeared between them. The stadium lights switched off a few minutes later. Mr. Tran swung the telescope toward the hills, adjusted the focus, and stepped back so Ines could look first.

Two stars, one gold and one faintly blue, sat side by side in the eyepiece. Her stubborn resolve to stay, Ines decided, had been worth every cold minute, and she wrote the time in her logbook in large, careful letters.$q$, 'draft')
