-- Passage rg10-evidence-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0ec8b0a6-0340-5453-893b-b7accb5a698a', 'rg10-evidence-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Catching Water from the Fog$q$, $q$On the dry coastal hills above the fishing village of Punta Arenilla, rain falls only a few days each year. Fog, however, rolls in from the ocean on most mornings. For generations, villagers noticed that the few shrubs on the ridges stayed green even in the driest months, and they guessed that the plants were somehow drinking the mist.

In 2016, engineer Tomasa Quispe and a group of residents decided to test that idea with fog nets. Each net is a tall rectangle of fine plastic mesh stretched between two poles and turned to face the wind. As fog drifts through the mesh, tiny droplets cling to the threads, merge into larger drops and run down into a gutter at the bottom. A pipe carries the water to a storage tank in the village.

The first season produced mixed results. Nets placed low on the slopes collected almost nothing, while nets on the highest ridge filled the tank faster than anyone expected. Quispe moved every net uphill the following year. By 2019, twelve nets supplied enough water for the village school's kitchen and a small vegetable plot, though families still relied on a delivery truck for drinking water.

The system needs steady care. Strong winds tear the mesh, and dust can clog the threads during long dry spells. Residents now take turns climbing the ridge each week to inspect and patch the nets. Quispe has said that the project succeeded less because of the technology than because the village agreed to share the work of keeping it running.$q$, 'draft')
