-- Passage rg10-evidence-inference-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e3405368-081a-51b6-b988-0898f1d0d17e', 'rg10-evidence-inference-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$When Every Town Kept Its Own Clock$q$, $q$In the 1850s, the river town of Halvering set its clocks by the sun. When the sun stood highest over the courthouse, the town bell rang noon. The town of Brookmoor, forty miles to the east, did the same, which meant its noon arrived about three minutes earlier. For most people this difference hardly mattered, since few traveled far enough in a day to notice.

The railroad changed that. When the Halvering and Eastern Line opened in 1861, its timetables listed departures in Halvering time, but each station along the route kept its own local hour. A passenger boarding in Brookmoor had to subtract minutes in her head to know when the train would actually arrive. Station clerks posted conversion tables, and travelers complained that the tables were often wrong. In 1863, two freight trains were scheduled onto the same single track because their crews read different clocks. They stopped within sight of each other, and no one was hurt, but the railroad's directors were alarmed.

Within a year, the line adopted a single standard time for every station, based on the clock in its Halvering office. Some Brookmoor residents objected at first. A local newspaper grumbled that the railroad was trying to own the sun. Yet merchants soon found it easier to promise deliveries, and the town council quietly reset the courthouse clock to match the station.

By 1880, nearly every town in the region had followed. Historians of the area note that the change came not from a law but from the practical needs of people who had begun to move goods and passengers faster than the sun appeared to move.$q$, 'draft')
