   $q$What can be inferred from the detail that Brookmoor's noon arrived about three minutes earlier than Halvering's?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Brookmoor's clocks were badly made and ran faster than Halvering's clocks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Brookmoor residents woke up earlier than people living in Halvering.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Because Brookmoor lies east of Halvering, the sun reached its highest point there sooner.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The railroad had already set Brookmoor's clocks before the line opened.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-time-period"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Because Brookmoor lies east of Halvering, the sun reached its highest point there sooner.$q$, $q$The passage says both towns set noon by the sun and that Brookmoor is "forty miles to the east," so the earlier noon reflects the sun's position, not faulty clocks. The railroad did not exist until 1861, so it could not explain the 1850s difference.$q$, 'draft', 'e3405368-081a-51b6-b988-0898f1d0d17e')
