-- Passage rg10-evidence-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2a608e7a-88c7-5ab8-b406-52d9b9a1ba2e', 'rg10-evidence-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Giving Buses the Green Light$q$, $q$In the city of Ostrava Bay, the number 14 bus once had a reputation for being late. Its route crossed downtown through twenty-two traffic signals, and drivers often waited through red light after red light while the bus filled with frustrated riders.

In 2022, the city transit office tried a system called signal priority on that route. Each bus carries a small radio transmitter. As a bus approaches an intersection, the transmitter sends a message to the traffic signal. If the light is about to turn red, the signal can hold the green for a few extra seconds. If the light is already red, it can shorten the red phase slightly. The system never changes a light instantly, and it does not work when a pedestrian has pressed the crossing button.

After one year, average travel time on route 14 fell from forty-one minutes to thirty-four. The share of buses arriving within two minutes of the schedule rose from fifty-eight percent to eighty-one percent. Ridership climbed by twelve percent, although the transit office notes that a fare discount for students began the same year.

Traffic engineer Ayesha Rahimi tracked the cross streets as well. Cars waiting on side streets were delayed by an average of four seconds per light, a change most drivers would hardly notice. Some business owners on those streets had worried that customers would avoid their shops, but sales records showed no clear drop. The city now plans to add signal priority to three more routes, starting with the ones that pass through the most intersections.$q$, 'draft')
