-- Passage rg10-figurative-language-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('248dda41-945b-504d-807d-6115bfb9063e', 'rg10-figurative-language-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Board Seven$q$, $q$The regional chess tournament was held in a school cafeteria that still smelled faintly of pizza. Forty boards stood in neat rows, and the only sounds were the soft click of pieces and the tap of hands on the clocks. It was the kind of deafening silence that made Tobias Lindqvist aware of every breath he took.

Across from him on board seven sat Priya Raman, his teammate during the school year and his opponent today. Their coach liked to call them friendly enemies, and the label fit. They ate lunch together every day, yet each of them kept a notebook of the other's favorite openings.

Priya moved her knight in the first minute, quick and sure. Tobias studied the board for a long time before answering. He was not slow, he told himself, only careful.

By the middle of the game, his position had grown uncomfortable. His bishop was trapped behind his own pawns, and Priya's rooks were lining up on the open file like guests waiting at a door. Tobias offered a draw. Priya shook her head politely and pushed another pawn.

Twenty moves later he tipped over his king and reached across to shake her hand. Afterward, the coach looked over their score sheet and said that Tobias had "found a creative way to give away a bishop." Tobias laughed, because he knew exactly what the coach meant.

On the bus home, Priya slid into the seat beside him and opened her notebook. "Show me what you were thinking on move fourteen," she said. For the first time all day, the rivalry felt like the friendship it had always been underneath.$q$, 'draft')
