-- Passage rg10-figurative-language-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2462c730-093c-517d-8801-94b1d288a8d5', 'rg10-figurative-language-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Safety Brake$q$, $q$In the middle of the nineteenth century, most people were understandably wary of riding in elevators. Hoisting platforms already existed in factories and warehouses, but they hung from a single rope. If that rope snapped, the platform and everything on it dropped straight down the shaft.

The breakthrough was not a stronger rope. It was a brake that worked when the rope failed. Inventors fitted the sides of the shaft with toothed rails and attached a spring to the top of the platform. As long as the rope was tight, the spring stayed bent. If the rope went slack, the spring snapped open and pushed metal hooks into the teeth, stopping the platform within a few inches.

Demonstrations of such brakes became a kind of theater. A builder would ride a platform high above a crowd, then order an assistant to cut the rope. The audience would gasp, the platform would lurch, and then it would hold. Spectators had watched what one writer of the period might have called a controlled fall, a drop that was frightening but never truly dangerous.

The brake changed more than machines. Once people trusted elevators, architects could design buildings far taller than anyone wanted to climb by stairs. Upper floors, once the least desirable rooms because of the long walk, became prized for their views and quiet.

Today, elevators use many layers of protection, and passengers rarely give them a second thought. That ease is itself a sign of success. The best safety inventions are the ones people eventually forget, because they have quietly done their job for so long.$q$, 'draft')
