values
  ('50a23229-60e7-5aac-adb5-cb7f162ed1e2', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$At the end, the father says the piano "is not old and it is not vintage. It is ours." How does this line develop the passage's focus on word choice?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It replaces words about the piano's age and value with a word about belonging earned through work.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It reveals that the family plans to sell the piano back to its owner.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It shows that the family regrets buying a piano that needed repairs.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"It proves that \"vintage\" was the most accurate word for the piano.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It replaces words about the piano's age and value with a word about belonging earned through work.$q$, $q$After a day of repairs, the father sets aside both "old" and "vintage," and Luis decides "ours" is "the best word of all" because it expresses pride and ownership. The line rejects "vintage" rather than proving it accurate.$q$, 'draft', '55e04f37-dcb7-5914-9a5b-d7401c418c67')
