values
  ('d7926a06-9fb3-5981-92d3-5b093600d296', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The author writes that the language is not "exactly dishonest" and that sellers choose "the most flattering words available." Which pair of words best shows the difference the author is describing?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Scratched\" and \"well-loved,\" which describe the same wear in plain and flattering ways.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Bicycle\" and \"bike,\" which are just a long and a short name for one thing.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"no-connotation-difference"},{"text":"\"Tire\" and \"wheel,\" which name two different parts of a bicycle.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"different-denotation"},{"text":"\"New\" and \"broken,\" which describe completely opposite conditions.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Scratched" and "well-loved," which describe the same wear in plain and flattering ways.$q$, $q$Flattering language keeps the same basic meaning while changing the feeling, exactly like "scratched" and "well-loved." "New" and "broken" would be an actual lie about condition, which the author says sellers are not telling.$q$, 'draft', '5881a7d5-59e2-52c6-94a7-1ea9049b07d8')
