-- Passage rg10-figurative-language-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2e36ce9a-2dca-5cff-a90b-752aeb4ce2be', 'rg10-figurative-language-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Dog with Personality$q$, $q$On her first Saturday volunteering at the county animal shelter, Amara Delacroix learned that the kennel cards had their own vocabulary. A dog described as "energetic" would probably pull hard on the leash. A cat listed as "independent" did not enjoy being held. And a dog with "a lot of personality" was usually a puzzle that nobody had solved yet.

That was how the card for Biscuit read. Biscuit was a stocky brown dog with one ear that stood up and one that flopped. He barked at every person who walked by his kennel, a loud, rolling bark that echoed off the concrete walls.

"Most visitors just keep walking," said Mr. Okonkwo, the volunteer coordinator. "They see the barking and decide he is aggressive. I think he is mostly lonely."

Amara noticed that the barking stopped whenever someone sat down on the floor near the gate. So she started spending her breaks there, reading a book out loud. By the third week, Biscuit greeted her with a wagging tail instead of a bark, and he would lean his heavy head against the bars while she read.

She asked Mr. Okonkwo if she could rewrite Biscuit's card. He agreed, and she thought carefully about each word. "Loud" became "talkative." "Stubborn" became "determined." She did not want to hide the truth, but she wanted visitors to see the same dog she had come to know.

Two weeks later, a family with a teenage son stopped at Biscuit's kennel and sat down on the floor. Biscuit went home with them that afternoon, his crooked ear bouncing as he trotted out the door.$q$, 'draft')
