values
  ('8e15d082-1ce4-5c7d-bec7-2f80408c71c2', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Mr. Okonkwo says visitors decide Biscuit is "aggressive," but he thinks Biscuit is "lonely." How do these two words differ in their effect on a visitor?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both words suggest a dangerous dog that visitors should avoid.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"Both words describe a dog that is quiet and rarely makes noise.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"\"Lonely\" suggests a dangerous dog, while \"aggressive\" invites sympathy.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-connotation"},{"text":"\"Aggressive\" suggests a threat, while \"lonely\" suggests a dog who needs company.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Aggressive" suggests a threat, while "lonely" suggests a dog who needs company.$q$, $q$The same barking is read two ways: "aggressive" pushes visitors away, while "lonely" invites them to help, which Amara's floor visits confirm. Biscuit "barked at every person," so the words do not describe a quiet dog.$q$, 'draft', '2e36ce9a-2dca-5cff-a90b-752aeb4ce2be')
