values
  ('a6f12e18-91df-5a06-a4ea-c99cc26ebdb7', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Many people called the plan "a gamble," while a few "more kindly" called it "a venture." What difference between these words does the author point out?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both describe a risky undertaking, but \"venture\" sounds bolder and more respectable.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Gamble\" means a business plan, while \"venture\" means a game of chance.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-connotation"},{"text":"\"Venture\" suggests the plan was guaranteed to succeed from the start.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-extreme"},{"text":"The words have completely different meanings and are not related at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"different-denotation"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Both describe a risky undertaking, but "venture" sounds bolder and more respectable.$q$, $q$Both words name a risk, but "gamble" suggests recklessness while "venture" suggests purposeful boldness, which is why the author says "more kindly." A venture still involves risk, so it does not mean guaranteed success.$q$, 'draft', 'a64d80fc-e58f-572f-aac4-f572782d3a6c')
