-- Passage rg10-literary-structure-pov-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1a270ac8-ac74-5c13-b42a-385aa713cf4c', 'rg10-literary-structure-pov-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Blue Vase$q$, $q$On the morning of the exhibition, the blue vase stood alone on a white pedestal near the gallery door, and a thin crack ran from its lip halfway down its side. Visitors slowed when they reached it. A few frowned. One man leaned close and said to his friend that someone must have dropped it. Nadia Okafor, seventeen and wearing her only good jacket, stood ten feet away and said nothing.

Three weeks earlier, the vase had been a glowing orange bulb on the end of a steel pipe. Mr. Varga, who ran the glass studio behind the hardware store, had warned her that the color she wanted would be difficult. "Cobalt cools unevenly," he said. "You will lose three before you keep one." She lost four. The fifth came out of the cooling oven whole, and she carried it to the shelf as if it were asleep.

The next afternoon, a student from the beginners' class bumped the shelf. The vase did not fall, but when Nadia picked it up she heard a sound like ice settling in a glass, and the crack appeared. She sat on the studio floor for a long time. Mr. Varga sat beside her and did not tell her it was fine.

That night she made a decision. She would not hide the crack, and she would not make a sixth vase. Instead, she wrote a small card and asked the gallery to place it beside the pedestal.

Now, in the gallery, the man who had blamed a careless visitor bent down and read the card. It said: "This vase cracked while cooling on a shelf. I kept it because the crack is the only part of it I did not plan." He stood up slowly, looked at the vase again, and then looked around the room as if searching for the person who had written that.$q$, 'draft')
