   $q$How does the flashback change the reader's understanding of the man who says someone "must have dropped it"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It shows that he is Mr. Varga in disguise, testing whether Nadia will be honest about the vase.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It shows the reader that his guess is wrong before he discovers it himself, creating suspense about his reaction.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It proves that he is the beginner student who bumped the shelf, which creates a surprise ending.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It confirms that his guess is correct, so the reader agrees with him when he reads the card.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-flashback"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows the reader that his guess is wrong before he discovers it himself, creating suspense about his reaction.$q$, $q$Because the flashback reveals the true cause (a bump on a shelf while cooling), readers know more than the man and wait to see how he responds. The text never links him to the beginner student.$q$, 'draft', '1a270ac8-ac74-5c13-b42a-385aa713cf4c')
