   $q$How does telling the story backward change the meaning of the stanza about "ordinary Tuesdays"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Because readers already know the family leaves, the plain routine feels precious instead of dull.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Because the stanza describes Denver, it shows the new home is less exciting than the old one.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Because the routine is listed quickly, it shows the speaker wanted to forget those years.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Because readers have not yet learned of the move, the routine seems like the start of the story.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-sequence"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Because readers already know the family leaves, the plain routine feels precious instead of dull.$q$, $q$By the time the Tuesdays appear, the reader has already seen the empty room and the key, so the "too normal to notice" life becomes something lost. The stanza describes the old apartment, not Denver.$q$, 'draft', 'dcbba9d9-b980-5112-92c2-67d4dceaf0a4')
