   $q$The poem ends with the four-year-old believing "this is where we live now." Why is this ending more powerful because of the poem's backward structure?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The reader sees that the mother lied, so the ending turns the poem into a criticism of her choices.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The reader learns for the first time that the family moves, so the ending works as a surprise twist.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"},{"text":"The reader knows that this home will be lost, so the child's trust becomes bittersweet rather than simply hopeful.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The reader returns to the key on the counter, so the ending repeats the opening image exactly.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The reader knows that this home will be lost, so the child's trust becomes bittersweet rather than simply hopeful.$q$, $q$Since the poem opened with leaving, the child's simple belief carries the reader's knowledge of its ending, creating a bittersweet close. The move was clear from the first stanza, so it is not a twist.$q$, 'draft', 'dcbba9d9-b980-5112-92c2-67d4dceaf0a4')
