-- Passage rg10-literary-structure-pov-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('66b84920-0178-539d-b1b0-048e495b9f43', 'rg10-literary-structure-pov-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Warp and Weft$q$, $q$My aunt Lorraine does not own a clock in her weaving room. She owns a loom taller than I am, a basket of wool dyed with sagebrush and onion skins, and a window that faces east. When I asked her once how long a rug takes, she looked at me the way you look at someone who has asked how long a river is.

I came to her house in June with a plan. My history teacher had assigned a project on family traditions, due in three weeks, and I figured I could learn the basics of Dine weaving in a week, film myself finishing a small piece, and be done. I even made a schedule on my phone with colored blocks.

On the first day, Aunt Lorraine did not let me touch the loom. We carded wool. On the second day, we carded wool. On the third day, she told me a story about her grandmother, who wove while herding sheep in her head, counting them in rows the way she counted threads. I wrote "no progress" in my schedule.

By the second week I was allowed to weave, and I wove badly. My edges pulled in like a waist. Aunt Lorraine did not fix them. She said the rug would remember every mistake and every good choice, and that this was not a problem. It was the point.

I did not finish a rug that summer. I turned in a project about carding wool, a story about sheep, and a crooked strip of red and gray that my teacher hung on the wall. What I did not put in the project was the moment, near the end, when I stopped checking my phone. I only noticed it later, when I saw that the colored blocks had stopped changing on the second Tuesday.$q$, 'draft')
