  ('b47c5883-067c-58ce-b051-cf651fcf92fc', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-literary-structure-pov'), null, 'medium',
   $q$What cultural view of making things does Aunt Lorraine express when she says the rug "would remember every mistake"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"A finished piece should hide its history, so mistakes must be removed before anyone sees it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"A finished piece must match a pattern exactly, so beginners should not be allowed to weave.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"A finished piece is valuable only when it is sold, so its history does not really matter.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"A finished piece carries the history of how it was made, so imperfections are part of its value.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A finished piece carries the history of how it was made, so imperfections are part of its value.$q$, $q$She says the remembered mistakes are "not a problem. It was the point," showing that the process and its traces give the rug meaning. She lets the narrator weave and does not fix the edges.$q$, 'draft', '66b84920-0178-539d-b1b0-048e495b9f43')
