   $q$Why does the narrator reveal the moment she stopped checking her phone only at the very end, and only through the schedule she noticed later?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It shows that she lost her phone on the second Tuesday, which explains why the project was incomplete.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It shows that the project deadline had passed, so she no longer needed to keep track of time at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It shows that her change was gradual and unnoticed even by her, which surprises the reader as it surprised her.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It shows that she was hiding her change from her aunt, who would have been angry about the schedule.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows that her change was gradual and unnoticed even by her, which surprises the reader as it surprised her.$q$, $q$The narrator says she "only noticed it later," so placing the detail last lets readers discover her shift in thinking the same delayed way she did. Nothing suggests the phone was lost; she simply stopped looking.$q$, 'draft', '66b84920-0178-539d-b1b0-048e495b9f43')
