-- Passage rg10-literary-structure-pov-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ed175c7e-8e4b-554a-986e-8657ca6d97cf', 'rg10-literary-structure-pov-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Box 114$q$, $q$The archive closed at five, but Teodora Lindqvist asked the night guard for one more hour. Box 114 had arrived that morning from an estate sale in the valley, and its label said only "Letters, 1931 to 1934, unknown."

The first letter was written in pencil on the back of a seed catalog page. The writer described a bridge being built over a river, the cables "spun like thread by men who never look down." He signed it with the initial T.

The second letter was about a dog that followed the work crew every morning and slept under the supply wagon. The third was about the cold. In the fourth, the writer mentioned a daughter he had not yet met, born in a town three hundred miles away while he was on the bridge. He asked his wife to describe the baby's hands.

Teodora set the fourth letter down carefully. She had cataloged thousands of letters, and she knew the rule: never become attached. Archivists record, they do not adopt. But she kept reading.

The letters grew shorter as the bridge grew longer. In the last one, dated October 1934, the writer said the final cable was in place and that he would be home by the first snow. He asked one more question: had the baby been named yet? He hoped, he wrote, that they might call her after his own mother.

There was no reply in the box. There was only, at the very bottom, a small photograph of a woman holding an infant on a porch, and on the back, in different handwriting: "Teodora, three months."

The archivist sat very still. Then she took out her phone and called her mother, who answered on the second ring and said, before anything else, "You found Grandpa's letters, didn't you."$q$, 'draft')
