-- Passage rg10-literary-structure-pov-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cf39a7c7-5f49-5b95-86ec-fb50ce48488b', 'rg10-literary-structure-pov-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Heron and the Calendar$q$, $q$My grandmother tells this story every year on the first warm night, while the frogs start up in the ditch behind her house. She tells it the same way each time, and if I interrupt, she starts over.

Once, she says, a young heron found a calendar that a farmer had left on a fence post. It was a fine calendar, with a picture of a mountain for every month, and the heron was impressed. She decided she would live by it.

She planned her days in neat squares. On the fourth of the month she would fish the north bank. On the ninth she would rest. On the fifteenth she would visit her cousin in the reeds. The other herons laughed, but the young heron said they were only jealous of her organization.

The calendar worked beautifully until the spring flood. The north bank went underwater on the third. The reeds washed downstream. By the fifteenth, her cousin had moved two miles away, and the fish had gone wherever fish go when the water turns brown. The young heron stood on her fence post holding a calendar full of plans for a river that no longer existed.

An old heron landed beside her. "The river does not read," he said. "Watch the water, and the water will tell you the date."

Here my grandmother always pauses. She looks at the frogs, or at the sky, or at me. Then she says the ending: the young heron did not throw the calendar away. She kept it, because the mountains were pretty.

For years I thought that was a joke she added to make me laugh. This year, I noticed that the calendar on her kitchen wall is six years out of date, still open to a picture of a mountain, and that she always knows exactly when the frogs will come.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
