-- Passage rg10-main-idea-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5c88cc63-fe99-595e-9c39-ef5b5a213fd2', 'rg10-main-idea-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Reading Sound with Light$q$, $q$Long before tape or digital files, some of the earliest sound recordings were cut into the surface of wax cylinders. A needle traced a groove that wiggled with the sound, and playing the cylinder meant running a needle through that groove again. The fictional Calloway Regional Archive holds about two thousand of these cylinders, including songs, speeches and family greetings recorded more than a century ago.

The archive faces a difficult choice. Many cylinders are cracked, moldy or so brittle that a single playing could destroy them. Yet if no one plays them, the sounds they hold will remain unheard, and the wax will keep slowly decaying on the shelf.

To escape this choice, the archive's engineer, Ananya Breckford, uses a method that never touches the groove. A camera with a powerful lens photographs the cylinder's surface in thousands of overlapping images as it turns slowly under a light. Software then builds a detailed three-dimensional map of the groove and calculates the sound that a needle would have produced. Because nothing scrapes the wax, even a cylinder broken into pieces can be scanned after the fragments are carefully fitted together.

The method has drawbacks. Scanning one cylinder can take several hours, and the resulting audio sometimes contains hiss where mold has filled the groove. Breckford's team also listens to every file and compares it with any surviving notes about the recording, because software can mistake a scratch for part of the sound.

Even so, the results have been striking. Recordings that had not been heard in a hundred years, including a lullaby sung by a local farmer and a speech at the opening of the county's first bridge, are now available online. A technology built for measuring tiny surfaces has given the archive a way to preserve its fragile collection without destroying it.$q$, 'draft')
