-- Passage rg10-main-idea-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('79e6575b-848f-55d7-8f2a-eb00e44be484', 'rg10-main-idea-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Calm Water, Faster Swims$q$, $q$Watch a swimming race from the stands and you might think the only thing slowing swimmers down is the water itself. In fact, swimmers also fight against waves, including the ones they create. Every stroke and kick pushes water outward, and in a crowded pool those waves bounce off walls and other swimmers. A pool that stays calm during a race is not an accident. It is the result of careful design.

The most visible feature is the lane line. Older lane dividers were simple ropes with floats, which marked the lanes but did little to stop waves. Modern racing lines are strings of plastic discs that spin as water hits them. The spinning breaks up waves and pushes their energy downward, so a wave from lane four loses much of its strength before it reaches lane five.

The edges of the pool matter too. In the fictional city of Port Sabelle, the new aquatic center was built with gutters that run along every wall at water level. When a wave reaches the side, it spills into the gutter instead of bouncing back toward the swimmers. The center's designer, Kwame Ostrander, also made the pool deeper than the old one, since waves that travel down into deep water have less force at the surface.

Coaches in Port Sabelle noticed the difference quickly. Swimmers in the outside lanes, who once complained of choppy water, reported smoother races. A study by the city's swim club found that times in the outer lanes moved closer to times in the middle lanes after the switch.

None of this makes a slow swimmer fast. Training, technique and strength still decide most races. But by reducing waves, pool designers help make sure the result depends more on the swimmers than on the lane they happened to draw.$q$, 'draft')
