-- Passage rg10-main-idea-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('09532bbe-2025-5fbf-af6d-ed587b3fd74f', 'rg10-main-idea-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Give Every Student a Field Notebook$q$, $q$Our science classes at Westbrook High spend a great deal of time on worksheets and screens. Students read about ecosystems, label diagrams of leaves and watch videos of animals in faraway places. All of this has value. But I believe something important is missing, and it could be added with a pencil and a few dollars' worth of paper. Every ninth and tenth grade science student should keep a field notebook and use it outside at least once a week.

A field notebook is simple. Students choose a spot, perhaps a corner of the school lawn or a tree by the parking lot, and return to it regularly. Each visit, they sketch what they see, note the weather and write down questions. Over months, the pages build a record of change: the first buds, the arrival of certain birds, the puddle that forms after every storm.

The strongest reason to keep these notebooks is that they teach students to observe carefully, which is where science begins. Drawing a leaf forces you to notice the shape of its edge and the pattern of its veins in a way that labeling a printed diagram does not. Last spring, during a pilot program at Westbrook, a student named Imani Castellanos sketched the same maple tree for ten weeks. She noticed that its north side leafed out days later than its south side, and she designed an experiment to find out why.

Some teachers worry that outdoor time takes away from material on the test. That is a fair concern. Yet a weekly visit can take as little as fifteen minutes, and the questions students raise outside can lead directly into lessons on climate, plant growth and the methods of research.

Screens and textbooks will always have a place in science class. Field notebooks would not replace them. They would give students what no screen can: the habit of looking closely at the world right outside the door.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
