-- Passage rg10-story-details-inference-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1f875b6b-f212-5b67-9fe0-7406cdfa38d6', 'rg10-story-details-inference-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Your Own Race$q$, $q$Keanu Alvarado had run the Ridgeline course enough times to know where it lied. The first mile was flat and wide, and everyone went out too fast on it. The second mile climbed through pine trees on a trail so narrow that passing was nearly impossible. The third mile came down the far side of the ridge, and that was where races were actually decided.

Coach Brandt had given the team one instruction at the start line: "Run your own race." Keanu had nodded with everyone else. Then the starter's horn sounded, and his teammate Femi, a freshman running his first varsity meet, sprinted to the front of the pack as if the finish line were around the first corner.

Keanu checked his watch at the first mile marker. He was eleven seconds slower than his usual split, and Femi was a small orange shirt far ahead. By the base of the hill, the orange shirt was getting larger. Halfway up, Keanu could hear Femi breathing, a ragged sound like a saw cutting wet wood.

He pulled even with him on the narrow trail. He did not pass. Instead he said, "Stay on my shoulder. Short steps." Femi did not answer, but he stayed. When the trail opened at the top, Keanu felt the familiar pull in his legs that meant he could go, and for a few strides he did, and the gap behind him opened. Then he slowed until the sound of Femi's breathing was beside him again.

They crossed the line fifty-second and fifty-third out of ninety. Keanu's time was the slowest he had run since the spring. Femi lay on the grass with his arms over his face, and Keanu went to find water for both of them.

Coach Brandt walked over holding his clipboard. He looked at the results, then at Keanu, for longer than he needed to. "That was not your own race," he said.

"No," Keanu said.

The coach wrote something on the clipboard, and the corner of his mouth moved in a way that was not quite a frown.$q$, 'draft')
