   $q$Which event from the fable best supports the weathervane's claim that the kite does not truly go where it likes?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The children shout with joy while holding the string.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"},{"text":"The north wind leaves the kite tangled in an oak.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The farmer's son lays the kite on a beam to dry.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"weak-evidence"},{"text":"The kite swoops low over the barn and shoots upward.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-claim"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The north wind leaves the kite tangled in an oak.$q$, $q$When the wind shifts, the kite ends up snapped and stuck in a tree, a place it surely did not choose. Its swoop over the barn is the kite's own attempt to prove the opposite claim.$q$, 'draft', 'c1a1a6bd-05b3-55c3-beb0-33d8d34d3ed7')
