-- Passage rg10-story-details-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a6a05ab9-b449-5c56-ae70-4422edf5b9f8', 'rg10-story-details-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Field Day$q$, $q$Every June the Carver Valley Radio Club set up its antennas in the high school parking lot for a twenty-four hour contest, and every June Ravi Mendoza's neighbor, Mrs. Ostrowski, asked him to help carry the equipment. This year, for the first time, she also asked him to sit at the radio.

The goal of the contest was simple to explain and hard to do: make contact with as many other stations as possible, using only power from batteries and a small generator. Each contact meant hearing another operator's call sign through the static, saying your own clearly, and writing both in the log before the signal faded.

Ravi's first hour was a disaster. He talked too fast, forgot to say the club's call sign twice, and once wrote down a station from Ohio as a station from Idaho. Mrs. Ostrowski sat beside him eating sunflower seeds and corrected nothing out loud. She only pointed at the log each time, and he would see the mistake and fix it himself.

Around midnight the conditions changed. Signals that had been faint came in strong, and suddenly operators from all over the country were calling at once. Ravi stopped worrying about how he sounded. He listened, answered, wrote, and listened again. When he finally looked up, the log showed ninety-one contacts in two hours, and Mrs. Ostrowski had fallen asleep in her folding chair with the bag of seeds still in her hand.

At four in the morning a weak voice came through, so faint that Ravi had to press the headphones against his ears. It was a station on an island in the Pacific, more than seven thousand miles away. He repeated the call sign three times to be sure, logged it, and sat back.

He did not wake Mrs. Ostrowski. But when the sun came up and she looked at the log, she ran her finger down the page until she found the island, and she stopped there and did not say anything for a long time. Then she took the pen and drew a small star beside it.$q$, 'draft')
